% map's better signature promises: if F is a function, map F is a function.
% `meal` is a func, so its body must be functional -- calling map with the
% deterministic `likes!` keeps the promise. `wrong-meal` is only a pred, so
% the checker lets it call map with the plain relation `likes`: the call
% still runs, it just leaves a choice point that backtracking walks into.

data guest.
data dish.
symb mario, anna guest.
symb pizza, pasta, gelato dish.

pred likes guest -> dish.            % relational: mario likes two dishes
likes mario pizza.
likes mario pasta.
likes anna gelato.

func likes! guest -> dish.           % functional: commit to the first
likes! G D :- likes G D, !.

func map (func A -> B), list A -> list B.
map _ [] [].
map F [X|XS] [Y|YS] :- F X Y, map F XS YS.

func meal list guest -> list dish.
meal Gs Ds :- map likes! Gs Ds.

pred wrong-meal list guest -> list dish.
wrong-meal Gs Ds :- map likes Gs Ds.

main :-
  meal [mario, anna] Ds, print "meal:" Ds,
  ( wrong-meal [mario, anna] Ds1, print "wrong-meal:" Ds1, fail
  ; true ).
